course: dictionary
lesson: 5617
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. «Lightning» is a noun with two. Its first meaning is the bright flash in the sky during a storm. Its second meaning is very fast, as in «as quick as lightning». Read the words around it to find out which meaning the writer means.
explain: «Lightning can strike the same place more than once.» The clue is strike: a flash can hit the ground, so this is the storm meaning. «He ran with lightning speed» is the fast meaning, because speed tells you how quick something is. Always ask: does this fit the sky, or does it fit how fast?
explain: Lightning and thunder come together, but they are not the same thing. «Light travels much faster than sound, so we see lightning before we hear thunder.» Count the seconds from the flash to the bang, then divide by 3 to get about how many kilometres away the storm is.
fact: Lightning is a huge spark of electricity between clouds, or between a cloud and the ground.
fact: Lightning heats the air so fast that the air bangs, and that bang is thunder.
fact: «Lightning» can also mean very fast, as in «lightning speed».
fact: Sound travels only about 1 kilometre in 3 seconds, so «counting from flash to bang tells you how far away the lightning is».
fact: When thunder roars, go indoors, and stay away from tall trees, open fields, water and metal.
exercise: «Lightning can strike the same place more than once.» What does lightning mean here? | Very fast, like a runner | A loud bang on the ground | A soft, gentle rain | *The bright flash in the sky during a storm
exercise: Ella says «lightning speed» means very slow. Is she right? | Yes, it means very slow and calm | *No, it means very fast | No, it means very loud | Yes, it means it is raining hard
exercise: A girl counts 6 seconds from flash to bang. About how far away is the lightning? | *About 2 kilometres | About 6 kilometres | About 60 kilometres | About 12 kilometres
test: What makes lightning? | The sun shining through the clouds | Strong wind blowing the rain sideways | Hot air rising from a road | *A huge spark of electricity in a storm
test: When thunder roars, what should you do? | Run to the middle of an open field | Stand under the tallest tree | *Go indoors | Swim in the lake
exam: Why does the boy in Florida leave the beach after he counts 12 seconds from flash to bang? | *The lightning is about 4 kilometres away, close enough to strike | He is bored of the sand | The sea has become too warm | He wants to hear more thunder
